Pushed `6fc1b3ae8`. Here's what perf actually told us — far more useful than the `UNLACE_TIMING` view.

## What perf showed (and what `UNLACE_TIMING` couldn't)

`UNLACE_TIMING` said: `TypeConstraintSolver` is the slow pass (1347 ms/call).
`perf record` said: **42% of total wall-clock is in `BTreeMap::Iter::next` / `Vec::from_iter` / `search_tree` on `SsaFunction::var_map`** — and that cost is *spread across many passes*, not concentrated in TypeConstraintSolver. Per-pass timing missed it because no single pass owns the cost; it's the BTreeMap iteration tax that every pass pays.

### Top frames (10,866 perf samples)

| % | function |
|--:|---|
| **23.4%** | `BTreeMap::Iter::next` on `var_map` |
| 13.2% | `SccpPass::run` |
| 11.7% | `ConditionSimplifyPass::run` |
| **11.2%** | `Vec::from_iter` (filtered var_map iteration) |
|  7.1% | `BTreeMap::search_tree` |
| 19.3% | `solve_polymorphic_constraints` (cross-cutting) |
| 13.0% | `type_constraint_solver::insert_lower` |

## The real bug

Four passes do this:
```rust
var_map.iter().find_map(|((bid, idx, slot), sv)| {
    if *sv == target && matches!(slot, VarnodeSlot::Output) {
        Some((*bid, *idx))
    } else { None }
})
```

That's an **O(n) linear scan to find an entry by value**. With a BTreeMap of thousands of entries called per-pass per-fixpoint-iteration, it's the dominant cost. The fix is a *reverse index*: add a sibling `BTreeMap<SsaVarnode, (BlockId, usize)>` kept in sync with `var_map`, expose `SsaFunction::def_of(sv)`, swap all four callers. Filed as **t135** — that's the next slice with a real >20% expected win.

## Lessons

1. **perf catches what timing-based profilers miss.** The cost was spread across passes; each pass looked moderate. Sampling exposes the shared substrate.
2. **Studying upstream rustc source was the right move.** Reading `~/RustProjects/forks/rust/library/alloc/src/collections/btree/map.rs` confirmed `extract_if(range, pred)` is the right primitive — and that `range_search` does an O(log n) descent — but also clarified that the *clear-by-range* function I rewrote was a tiny fraction of the cost, while the *reverse-lookup* pattern is the real bug. Reading the source kept me from optimizing the wrong thing.
3. **Honest negative result is documented.** The `extract_if` rewrite is cleaner code but didn't move the needle on `/usr/bin/wc` — the function it speeds up is rarely on the hot path. I kept the rewrite (it's a strict improvement in code clarity + allocation count) and documented why it didn't show up in wall-clock.

## Three-tier profiling stack (now complete)

```
[--stats]               → finds slow function    ~1 second  ✅
[UNLACE_TIMING=1]       → finds slow pass        ~1 second  ✅
[cargo flamegraph]      → finds slow line of code ~1 minute  ✅ NEW
```

All three documented in `docs/profiling.md`. The three required `CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_*` env-var overrides are written down so the next person doesn't have to discover that your `~/.cargo/config.toml` strips symbols.

